 ## Handle mouse input
 
-Fullscreen mode routes normalized mouse events to components. A handler can mark an event handled, capture a drag sequence, request focus, or request a render. Components shown in an editor border slot (`ctx.ui.setWidget` with a border placement) receive events on their own columns of the border line, with coordinates local to the visible content. A border component may also implement `renderCompact(width)`, a shorter one-line form shown when its full first line does not fit its share of the border.
+Fullscreen mode and regular mode with `regularMouse: true` route normalized mouse events to components. A handler can mark an event handled, capture a drag sequence, request focus, or request a render. Components shown in an editor border slot (`ctx.ui.setWidget` with a border placement) receive events on their own columns of the border line, with coordinates local to the visible content. A border component may also implement `renderCompact(width)`, a shorter one-line form shown when its full first line does not fit its share of the border.
 
-Unhandled wheel events scroll the nearest `ScrollView`. Unhandled primary-button drags remain available for transcript selection. OSC 8 links take precedence over enclosing click regions.
+In fullscreen mode, unhandled wheel events scroll the nearest `ScrollView`. Unhandled primary-button drags remain available for transcript selection. OSC 8 links take precedence over enclosing click regions.
 
-Regular mode leaves mouse input to the terminal because the terminal owns scrollback. Design every interaction with a keyboard path even when fullscreen mouse input is available.
+Regular mode leaves mouse input to the terminal by default. Set `regularMouse: true` in settings and restart to enable component clicks. This captures mouse input terminal-wide: native drag selection and scrollback wheel handling are only guaranteed with the setting off. Regular capture does not add fullscreen transcript selection or copy-on-select. Clicks wait until terminal geometry is known; keyboard input and rendering continue while waiting. Design every interaction with a keyboard path even when mouse input is available.
 
 ## Use custom screens and overlays
 
