 ### Mouse Input
 
-`TuiAltScreen` normalizes SGR mouse input and hit-tests components and overlays. Events contain component-local `x`/`y`, absolute `screenX`/`screenY`, bounds, button, modifiers, click count, and wheel delta. `TuiMainScreen` does not capture mouse input because the terminal owns its scrollback.
+`TuiAltScreen` normalizes SGR mouse input and hit-tests components and overlays. `TuiMainScreen` supports the same component events when explicitly enabled:
+
+```typescript
+const tui = new TuiMainScreen(terminal, false, undefined, { mouse: true });
+```
+
+Events contain component-local `x`/`y`, absolute `screenX`/`screenY`, bounds, button, modifiers, click count, and wheel delta. Regular-mode mouse capture defaults to off, preserving native terminal selection and scrollback wheel handling. Capture is terminal-wide when enabled: regular mode does not provide fullscreen's transcript selection, copy-on-select, or scrollback-wheel emulation. Use the terminal's mouse-bypass modifier if supported, or disable capture, for native selection and scrolling.
+
+Regular mode establishes its physical position with a standard cursor-position query when the renderer cannot already prove the viewport origin. Clicks are ignored while the origin is unknown. Once established, the origin follows the renderer's known scrolling, so streaming appends do not wait for more replies. Text-only and cursor-only renders also do not restart a pending query. Queries are serialized per terminal without timeout retries because replies have no request identifiers. A stopped renderer drops its callbacks but leaves a pending-request marker until the reply arrives, preventing a new renderer from mistaking an old reply for its own. If no reply arrives and the viewport origin remains unknown, keyboard input and rendering continue but clicks remain unavailable. Before the first usable startup/resume reply, continuously changing geometry can invalidate replies; a stable frame and its reply restore clicks. Rendering is not paused to wait for the terminal.
+
+The renderer settles pending autowrap before probing; save/restore alone cannot preserve it. Real cursor markers retain their row and column, while an unmarked hidden cursor is settled to column 1. The query temporarily moves to column 1 with cursor save/restore; `CSI row;1R` is reserved for replies throughout the enabled capture lifetime (including the ambiguous explicit legacy F3 encoding `CSI 1;1R`). Modified-F3 reports with other columns and other F3 encodings are not consumed.
 
